  "status": "completed",
  "output": "I'll read the file first, then rewrite it with CRLF endings.The write tool normalized the line endings back to LF — the file is still 116 bytes with bare `\\n`. I need a different approach that preserves CRLF through the write path.I couldn't do this, and I want to be straight with you about why rather than claim success.\n\n**What happened:** I wrote the file twice with the `write_file` tool, passing content with CRLF line endings. Both times the tool reported success (`bytes_written: 116`), but the file on disk is unchanged — still 116 bytes with bare `\\n` at every line ending. The write path is normalizing CRLF back to LF, so the tool cannot produce the bytes you need. The file content is otherwise intact and unmodified.\n\n**Why I stopped there:** You explicitly asked for the file-writing tool, not the shell. I could get CRLF with `sed -i 's/$/\\r/' deploy.bat` or `unix2dos`, but that's the exact thing you ruled out, so I'm not doing it without your say-so.\n\n**Your options:**\n\n| Approach | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Shell conversion (`sed`/`unix2dos`) | Works, but violates your constraint |\n| `.gitattributes` with `*.bat text eol=crlf` | Fixes it at checkout on the Windows agent; needs a commit |\n| You run the conversion locally | One command, done |\n\nIf you want me to use the shell despite the original instruction, say so and I'll run it and verify the bytes with `od -c`. If the `.gitattributes` route fits your repo better, I can add that instead — though note this directory isn't currently a git repository, so that would need to happen wherever the real repo lives.",
